<!--
    narrowret: a call reads the narrow value its callee returns (x86-64).

    The image is decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/narrowret.c
    (gcc -O2), its calls resolved at 0x1000.  `whole` returns `lea eax,
    [rdi+rdi*4]`: a 32-bit write, which clears the upper half of rax, and the
    caller `returns_word` reads rax whole after the call.  The listing
    declares `whole` an `int`, and the write proves a zero extension but no
    sign, so even under `decompile-all` it states nothing and `returns_word`
    keeps rax whole (#1, #2).  gcc's `next_byte` returns `lea eax, [rdi+1]`
    without extending the byte: nothing proves its upper bits zero, so it
    states nothing either and `larger_byte` reads `al` (#3).

    THIS FIXTURE IS THE NEGATIVE CONTROL, as kuna-narrowret.xml is for the
    Thumb build: this harness decompiles ONE function, so no callee states
    anything and both passes print the same.  The positive witnesses are
    tests/cli/narrowret-holds-a-narrow-call-result-narrow.json and its off
    arm over the Thumb object, and decompiler/crates/kuna-cli/tests/narrow_ret.rs
    over both objects.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "output-prototype".  Pass 1 (default on) and pass 2
    (off) must print the same.
-->
